config CC_OPTIMIZE
	bool "Enable compiler optimizations"
	default y if !DEBUG
	help
	  Say Y if you want to enable debug functions, such as
	  sys_dbg.
	  If you say N here, those functions are not compiled.

choice
	prompt "Optimize "
	depends on CC_OPTIMIZE

config CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SPEED
        bool "for speed"
        depends on CC_OPTIMIZE
        help
          Enabling this option will pass "-O2" instead of "-Os" to
          your compiler resulting in a faster code.

          If unsure, say y

config CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE
	bool "for size"
	depends on CC_OPTIMIZE
	help
	Enabling this option will pass "-Os" instead of "-O2" to
	your compiler resulting in a smaller code.

	If unsure, say N
endchoice

config THREADS
	bool "Enable Threading support"
	default y
	help
	  Enable multithreading primitives in the hpc runtime. Features that
	  provide concurrent, lockless access to shared data structures (such
	  as RCU) build on top of this.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config RCU
	bool "Enable (RCU) Read-Write-Update"
	default n
	depends on THREADS
	help
	  Efficient data structures based on RCU and lock-free algorithms.
          This data synchronization provides userland read-side access which 
          scales linearly with the number of cores

config CC_RSEQ
	bool "Enable (RSEQ) Restartable sequences"
	default n
	help
	  Restartable sequences are small segments of user-space code designed 
          to access per-CPU data structures without the need for heavyweight locking

choice
	prompt "CPU hardware acceleration"
	default CC_CPU_ACCEL_RUNTIME
	help
	  Select how CPU hardware acceleration (optimized assembly using
	  SIMD / crypto instruction-set extensions) is chosen.

	  Disabled       - portable C only; runs on any CPU, no acceleration.
	  Build-time     - fixed to the configured "Processor family"; fastest
	                   and smallest, but the binary requires a CPU at least
	                   as capable as that target (no runtime checks).
	  Runtime        - detect the host CPU at startup (CPUID / hwcaps) and
	                   pick the best available code path; runs correctly on
	                   any CPU of the architecture.

config CC_CPU_ACCEL_NONE
	bool "Disabled (portable C only)"
	help
	  No CPU-specific acceleration. Only generic C implementations are
	  built. The result runs on any CPU of the target architecture.

config CC_CPU_ACCEL_BUILDTIME
	bool "Build-time (fixed to target CPU)"
	help
	  Acceleration is selected at build time from the configured
	  "Processor family". Only instruction-set extensions guaranteed by
	  that target are used, with no runtime checks. Fastest and smallest,
	  but the binary requires a CPU at least as capable as the configured
	  target (it may fault with an illegal instruction on an older CPU).

config CC_CPU_ACCEL_RUNTIME
	bool "Runtime CPU detection"
	help
	  A self-dispatching implementation is built and the best code path
	  supported by the host CPU is selected at run time (via CPUID /
	  hwcaps, populated by a startup constructor). The binary runs
	  correctly on any CPU of the architecture and automatically uses the
	  extensions that are actually present, at a small one-time detection
	  cost.

	  If unsure, choose this.

endchoice

# Backwards-compatibility alias: true whenever any acceleration is enabled.
# Existing `depends on CC_CPU_ACCELERATION` throughout the tree keep working.
config CC_CPU_ACCELERATION
	def_bool CC_CPU_ACCEL_BUILDTIME || CC_CPU_ACCEL_RUNTIME

config CC_GPU_ACCELERATION
	bool "Enable GPU acceleration"
	default n
	help
	  Say Y if you want to enable GPU acceleration.

config CC_PIC
	bool "Enable Position-independent code"
	default n
	help
	Position-independent code (PIC) is code that can execute regardless of
	its absolute address (i.e. by using relative addressing).
	PIC is used for shared libraries, allowing library code to be located
	anywhere in memory.

config CC_STRIP
	bool "Strip executables"
	default y
	help
	  Strip all symbols and debug information from executables and
	  shared libraries during the build. This significantly reduces
	  binary size but makes debugging impossible.

	  If unsure, say N.

config CC_STDLIB
	bool "Use the standard system startup libraries"
	default y
	help
	  Use the standard system startup libraries when linking.

config CC_CLIB
	bool "Use the C library"
	default y
	help
	  Use the C library when linking. When disabled, still link with the startup files.

config SILENT
        bool "Enable Silent Mode"
        default n
        help
          Say Y for a build that says nothing about itself: the log functions,
          the trace points and the measurement instrumentation below are all
          turned off and cannot be turned back on while this is set.

          It is one switch rather than three because they are one decision -
          an image that is configured by whoever builds it, and never read by
          whoever runs it, has no use for any of them - and because leaving
          one of the three on by accident is how a "silent" build ends up
          carrying a metric namespace or a log archive it never emits.

          Say N and each of them is a choice of its own again.

config MEASURE
        bool "Enable Measurements"
        default y
        depends on !SILENT
        help
          Say Y if you want to enable measurements.

          This turns on the instrumentation half of <hpc/measure.h>: the
          storage pointer a subsystem counts through, the measure_inc() and
          friends that bump it, and the metric names and descriptions the
          reports are labelled with. With it off all of that costs nothing -
          no field, no branch, no string.

          Metrics the logic itself reads (a gauge like the slab's committed
          level is an input to the algorithm, not an observation of it) are
          declared with the always_measure_* family instead, and stay
          compiled in either way. Only their names and descriptions follow
          this symbol.

config MEASURE_HISTORY
        int "Measurement history depth (rows, 0 = none)"
        depends on MEASURE
        default 64
        range 0 65536
        help
          How many snapshots one measurement history keeps - the number of
          rows in the ring that DEFINE_MEASURE_HISTORY() reserves. When the
          ring is full the oldest row is overwritten, so a history costs a
          fixed amount of memory: depth * sizeof(struct <ns>_measure) plus a
          timestamp per row.

          0 keeps no history at all, and not as an empty ring: the rings, the
          sampler and every loop over rows compile away to nothing. The API
          stays - the history type keeps the live struct a subsystem counts
          through, and every measure_history_* macro keeps its shape - so the
          code that uses it is not touched, only removed.

          64 rows at the default interval is a bit under eleven minutes of
          recent behaviour, which is what a trend (is the slab still growing?
          did the hit rate fall off?) needs to be read from.

          Needs CONFIG_MEASURE: without it the symbol goes and the header's
          own default stands in. Callers that want no history simply do not
          instantiate one.

config MEASURE_HISTORY_INTERVAL
        int "Measurement history interval (seconds)"
        depends on MEASURE
        default 10
        range 1 86400
        help
          Seconds between history rows. measure_history_tick(), which the
          application calls from a loop it already has, saves a row only once
          this much time has passed since the last one; every other call is a
          compare and a return.

          Shorter resolves bursts, longer covers more time in the same number
          of rows - the ring holds CONFIG_MEASURE_HISTORY * this many seconds
          of the recent past either way.

config LOGGING
        bool "Enable Log Functions"
        default y
        depends on !SILENT
        help
          Say Y if you want to enable log functions, such as
          print_dbg, print_info, print_warn, print_err.
          If you say N here, those functions are not compiled.

config VERBOSE
        int "Default log verbosity level (0..4)"
        depends on LOGGING
        default 2
        range 0 4
        help
          The verbosity a build starts at: the value log_verbose is
          initialised to, which is what the debug1() .. debug4() gates in
          <hpc/log.h> compare against. 0 keeps every debug level quiet, 4
          lets all four through; the levels above them (info, warning, error)
          are not gated by it and always print.

          This is a default, not a limit. A program that has a verbosity
          option of its own - -v/--verbose - assigns log_verbose from it after
          parsing, so the command line still decides what a run says. What
          this sets is what is said before that point, which is where a
          configuration error or a failure to reach a device is reported, and
          what a program without such an option says at all.

          Needs CONFIG_LOGGING: with logging off nothing consults it.

config TRACE
        bool "Enable Trace Functions"
        default y
        depends on !SILENT
        help
          Say Y if you want to enable trace functions.

config CC_STACKPROTECTOR
	def_bool n
	help
	Set when a stack-protector mode is enabled, so that the build
	can enable support for the GCC feature.

choice
	prompt "Stack Protector buffer overflow detection"
	depends on HAVE_CC_STACKPROTECTOR
	default CC_STACKPROTECTOR_NONE
	help
	This option turns on the "stack-protector" GCC feature. This
	feature puts, at the beginning of functions, a canary value on
	the stack just before the return address, and validates
	the value just before actually returning.  Stack based buffer
	overflows (that need to overwrite this return address) now also
	overwrite the canary, which gets detected and the attack is then
	neutralized via a kernel panic.

config CC_STACKPROTECTOR_NONE
	bool "None"
	help
	Disable "stack-protector" GCC feature.

config CC_STACKPROTECTOR_REGULAR                                                
	bool "Regular"                                                          
	select CC_STACKPROTECTOR                                                
	help                                                                    
	Functions will have the stack-protector canary logic added if they    
	have an 8-byte or larger character array on the stack.                

	This feature requires gcc version 4.2 or above, or a distribution     
	gcc with the feature backported ("-fstack-protector").                

	On an x86 "defconfig" build, this feature adds canary checks to       
	about 3% of all kernel functions, which increases kernel code size    
	by about 0.3%.

config CC_STACKPROTECTOR_STRONG                                                 
	bool "Strong"                                                           
	select CC_STACKPROTECTOR                                                
	help                                                                    
	Functions will have the stack-protector canary logic added in any     
	of the following conditions:                                          
					                                                                                    
	- local variable's address used as part of the right hand side of an  
	assignment or function argument                                     
	- local variable is an array (or union containing an array),          
	regardless of array type or length                                  
	- uses register local variables                                       

	This feature requires gcc version 4.9 or above, or a distribution     
	gcc with the feature backported ("-fstack-protector-strong").         

	On an x86 "defconfig" build, this feature adds canary checks to       
	about 20% of all package functions, which increases the code    
	size by about 2%.                                                     
	
endchoice

config MODULES
	bool "Enable loadable module support"
	depends on CC_PIC
	default n
	option modules
	help
	  Modules are small pieces of compiled code which can be loaded
	  and unloaded into the running system. This allows extending
	  functionality without rebuilding the entire project.

	  If you say N here, all modules in the modules/ directory will
	  be compiled statically into the main binary with full inlining
	  for zero overhead.

	  If unsure, say N.

config MODVERSIONS
        bool "Module versioning support"
	depends on MODULES
	default n
        help
          Usually, you have to use modules compiled with your package.
          Saying Y here makes it sometimes possible to use modules
          compiled for different packages, by adding enough information
          to the modules to (hopefully) spot any changes which would
          make them incompatible with the software you are running.  If
          unsure, say N.

config MODULE_SRCVERSION_ALL
        bool "Source checksum for all modules"
	depends on MODULES
        help
          Modules which contain a MODULE_VERSION get an extra "srcversion"
          field inserted into their modinfo section, which contains a
          sum of the source files which made it.  This helps maintainers
          see exactly which source was used to build a module (since
          others sometimes change the module source without updating
          the version).  With this option, such a "srcversion" field
          will be created for all modules.  If unsure, say N.

